    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-impute" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Counting free grain and goods raises rural spending by up to 7%, most in Chhattisgarh and West Bengal</h3>
            <div class="sub">Rural India, 2023-24: how much higher a person's monthly spending is when the value of food and goods received free through government schemes is counted ("with imputation"), by state, per cent and ₹. States above the all-India rural figure are in blue.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-impute" set:html={TX["pv-impute"]}></div><div class="c">added, rural All India</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-impute" set:html={CH.impute()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, HCES 2023-24, MPCE with and without imputation, by state. The survey values free items at local prices; the public distribution system's free grain is most of it.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://esankhyiki.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The headline MPCE counts only what a household paid for. Many households also receive rice or wheat free from the ration shop, and some receive school uniforms, laptops or cycles. The survey puts a value on those and publishes a second figure "with imputation". The gap between the two is a rough measure of how much the state's welfare reaches the plate.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2023-24</span>
            <p>Most added: <b id="ex-imp-hi" set:html={TX["ex-imp-hi"]}></b>. All India, rural: <b id="ex-imp-ai" set:html={TX["ex-imp-ai"]}></b> a person a month; urban: <b id="ex-imp-aiu" set:html={TX["ex-imp-aiu"]}></b>. In <b id="ex-imp-zero" set:html={TX["ex-imp-zero"]}></b> states and union territories the two figures are the same.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-ginist" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Spending is more unequal in towns than in villages in 31 of 36 states</h3>
            <div class="sub">Gini coefficient of per-capita consumption spending, rural (blue) and urban (grey), by state, 2023-24, without imputation. 0 means everyone spends the same; higher means more unequal. Labels read urban / rural.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-ginist" set:html={TX["pv-ginist"]}></div><div class="c">states where the town is more unequal</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-ginist" set:html={CH.ginist()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, HCES 2023-24, Gini coefficient of per capita consumption expenditure by state and sector. Both conventions and both rounds are in the data.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://esankhyiki.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Each row is a state, with a dot for its villages and one for its towns. A Gini of spending runs lower than a Gini of income or wealth, because the poor spend most of what they have and the rich save, so these figures understate inequality. Compare states with each other, not with income Ginis published elsewhere.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2023-24</span>
            <p>Most unequal: <b id="ex-gs-hi" set:html={TX["ex-gs-hi"]}></b>. Least: <b id="ex-gs-lo" set:html={TX["ex-gs-lo"]}></b>. All India: rural <b id="ex-gs-air" set:html={TX["ex-gs-air"]}></b>, urban <b id="ex-gs-aiu" set:html={TX["ex-gs-aiu"]}></b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
