  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">10</span><h2>How to misread this number</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The average family lives on <b id="mr-rural" set:html={TX["mr-rural"]}></b> a month."</h3>
          <p>MPCE is per <em>person</em>, not per household. A rural family of five at the average spends about <b id="mr-fam" set:html={TX["mr-fam"]}></b> a month. Still modest: but a different number, and the one to use when the headline gets compared to a salary.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Spending has doubled since 2011-12."</h3>
          <p>The survey was redesigned after 2011-12: new questionnaire, new recall structure, new item list. The old rounds measure a different thing and are not served alongside these. Any line drawn across the redesign is a comparison the survey's own designers refuse to make; this guide refuses it too.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Urban India is <b id="mr-ratio" set:html={TX["mr-ratio"]}></b> richer."</h3>
          <p>The gap is in nominal spending, not welfare. City prices, above all rent, are higher, so a rupee buys less there; and spending is not income in either place. The real gap in living standards is smaller than the raw ratio, though how much smaller depends on a price deflator this survey does not provide.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iv.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The Gini fell, so inequality is solved."</h3>
          <p>This is a consumption Gini from a household survey: it misses income, misses savings, and undercounts the very top, which is where Indian inequality concentrates. The fall between two comparable rounds is real evidence: of spending compression, over one year, above a floor. That is all it is evidence of.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">v.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"With-imputation is the real number."</h3>
          <p>Neither convention is more real; they answer different questions. Without imputation counts what households actually paid: the cash-economy figure. With imputation prices in the free welfare goods they consumed: the living-standard figure. The headline convention is the first. Quoting the second without saying so moves the goalposts by a footnote.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

